# UST.G3DR H02 Five-Prime Reconstruction Holdout Theorem v1

## Status

`CLOSED_EXACT_FIVE_PRIME_RECONSTRUCTION_WITH_INDEPENDENT_SIXTH_PRIME_HOLDOUT`

G3DR crosses the signed-64-bit boundary with a native signed-128-bit exact
executor. It reconstructs each of the 46,032,168 normalized H02 kernel
coefficients from five complete split-prime archives, then tests the candidate
against the independently reserved sixth prime 69,011.

## Exact certificate

The executor uses sequential CRT and extended-Euclidean rational
reconstruction inside

```text
|numerator| <= floor(sqrt((M-1)/2)),
1 <= denominator <= floor(sqrt((M-1)/2)).
```

It persists separate candidate and holdout-pass masks for every coefficient.
All nonzero passing fractions are emitted with their canonical global index,
signed numerator and positive denominator. Zero passes remain implicit in the
pass mask.

The sixth prime is not used until all five-prime candidates are fixed.

## Boundary

This is exact finite reconstruction and out-of-sample validation. It is not a
height theorem or an identity over `Q(gamma)`. G3DR does not prove the
characteristic-zero rank, the second Woodbury factorization, source
contraction, path integration, integral marking, readout or `beta_C`.

The next stage uses the same 128-bit executor with six construction primes and
reserves prime 72,271 as the seventh-prime holdout.

## Reproduction

```powershell
python build_ust_g3dr_h02_five_prime_reconstruction_holdout.py
```

The packet is
`state/ust_g3dr_h02_five_prime_reconstruction_holdout.packet.json`; masks,
nonzero fractions and the native log are under `evidence/ust_g3dr/`.
